\section{Introduction}

The field of artificial intelligence has experienced significant growth in recent years, driven by advancements in machine learning and deep learning \cite{lecun2015deep}. A key research area within this domain is the development of efficient algorithms for solving complex optimization problems, which is a fundamental challenge in many fields, including computer science, operations research, and engineering \cite{bertsekas2018convex}. Optimization problems can be broadly categorized into three research lines: gradient-based methods, gradient-free methods, and hybrid approaches \cite{spall2003introduction}.

Gradient-based methods, such as gradient descent and quasi-Newton methods, rely on gradient computations to search for the optimal solution \cite{nocedal2006numerical}. In contrast, gradient-free methods, including evolutionary algorithms and swarm intelligence, do not require gradient information and are often used for non-convex or non-differentiable problems \cite{eiben2003introduction}. Hybrid approaches combine elements of both gradient-based and gradient-free methods to leverage their respective strengths \cite{rao2009engineering}. Despite significant progress in optimization techniques, existing methods still face challenges such as convergence to local optima, sensitivity to initial conditions, and high computational cost \cite{floudas2013handbook}.

The limitations of existing methods and the complexity of real-world optimization problems motivate the need for new approaches. Many real-world optimization problems involve multiple objectives, constraints, and uncertainties, making them challenging to solve \cite{marler2004survey}. The relationship between different research lines is complex, and each approach has its strengths and weaknesses. For instance, gradient-based methods are often more efficient than gradient-free methods but may not be suitable for non-convex problems \cite{boyd2004convex}. Gradient-free methods can handle non-convex problems but may be computationally expensive \cite{eiben2003introduction}.

This work aims to address the identified challenges by developing a new optimization algorithm that combines elements of machine learning and deep learning with traditional optimization techniques. The proposed algorithm will be designed to handle large-scale, non-convex, and multi-objective problems, and will incorporate uncertainty and robustness to handle real-world applications. The key research question is how to develop optimization algorithms that can efficiently handle complex optimization problems and incorporate uncertainty and robustness. The proposed approach will be evaluated using a range of benchmarks and test cases to demonstrate its effectiveness.

The contributions of this work are as follows:
\begin{itemize}
\item A new optimization algorithm that combines machine learning and deep learning with traditional optimization techniques.
\item An approach to incorporating uncertainty and robustness into optimization algorithms.
\item An evaluation of the proposed algorithm using a range of benchmarks and test cases.
\item An analysis of the proposed algorithm's strengths and weaknesses, including computational efficiency, convergence properties, and robustness to uncertainty.
\end{itemize}
These contributions will advance the state-of-the-art in optimization techniques and provide a more efficient and robust approach to solving complex optimization problems.